ndings like acanthosis nigricans."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of documented weight loss or hyperkeratosis in the physical exam weaken the case for glucagonoma syndrome?", "summary": "Absence of these features reduces but doesn't eliminate support for glucagonoma syndrome."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of central obesity, buffalo hump, and purple striae in the physical exam rule out hypercortisolism as the primary mechanism?", "summary": "Classic Cushing's features are absent, strongly excluding endogenous hypercortisolism."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of a painful erythematous rash with papules/plaques support secondary cutaneous infections (e.g., candidiasis) as a manifestation of diabetes mellitus in this patient?", "summary": ""}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of classic type 1 diabetes features (e.g., recent weight loss) and non-specific rash pattern weaken the likelihood of diabetes mellitus as the primary mechanism?", "summary": ""}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "Severe hyperglycemia (>500 mg/dL) with systemic constitutional symptoms (fatigue, malaise) and GI involvement (abdominal pain, diarrhea) suggests an acute hyperglycemic crisis requiring urgent stabilization.", "required_actions": ["Initiate intravenous insulin infusion", "Obtain serum electrolytes and blood gas analysis", "Start fluid resuscitation with isotonic saline"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Emergency interrupt is active (severe hyperglycemia, systemic symptoms, and GI involvement) but suppressed in static_diagnosis_qa mode. No branch meets confirmation criteria (posterior >= 0.75). Multiple child branches under B1 remain unresolved with distinct etiologies (type 1, glucagonoma) requiring further discrimination."}, "turn_budget_used": 2, "estimated_rema